{"execute":{"doc":1,"cmd":{"Write":{"PasteMarkdown":{"text":"Roast squash and sage risotto\n\nA slow, comforting autumn supper. Roasting the squash first gives it a sweet, caramelised edge that stirs through the rice.\n\n| Serves | Preparation | Cooking | Difficulty |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 4 | 20 minutes | 45 minutes | Easy |\n\n# Ingredients\n\n- 1 small butternut squash (about 900 g), peeled and cut into 2 cm cubes\n- 3 tbsp olive oil\n- 1.2 litres hot vegetable stock\n- 30 g butter\n- 1 onion, finely chopped\n- 2 garlic cloves, crushed\n- 300 g risotto rice\n- 150 ml dry white wine\n- 12 sage leaves\n- 60 g Parmesan-style hard cheese, finely grated, plus extra to serve\n\n# Method\n\n1. Heat the oven to 200 °C (180 °C fan). Toss the squash with 2 tbsp oil, season and roast for 30 minutes until golden at the edges.\n2. Meanwhile, melt half the butter in a wide pan and soften the onion for 8 minutes. Add the garlic and rice and stir for 2 minutes until the grains turn glossy.\n3. Pour in the wine and let it bubble away. Add the stock a ladle at a time, stirring often, until the rice is creamy but still has a little bite, about 20 minutes.\n4. Fry the sage leaves in the remaining oil until crisp, then drain on kitchen paper.\n5. Stir half the squash, the rest of the butter and the cheese into the risotto. Cover and rest for 2 minutes.\n6. Serve topped with the remaining squash, the crisp sage and extra cheese.\n\n# Cook's notes\n\nKeep the stock at a gentle simmer beside the risotto so that each ladle goes in hot. For a vegan version, use olive oil instead of butter and a plant-based hard cheese.\n"}}}}}
